    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Given that \\( n \\) when divided by 6 leaves a remainder of 1, we can express \\( n \\) in the form:\n\\[ n = 6k + 1 \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\).\n\nWe need to find the remainder when \\( n + 2010 \\) is divided by 6. First, substitute \\( n = 6k + 1 \\) into the expression \\( n + 2010 \\):\n\\[ n + 2010 = (6k + 1) + 2010 = 6k + 2011. \\]\n\nNext, we need to determine the remainder when \\( 6k + 2011 \\) is divided by 6. Since \\( 6k \\) is a multiple of 6, it contributes 0 to the remainder when divided by 6. Therefore, we only need to consider the remainder when 2011 is divided by 6:\n\\[ 2011 \\div 6 = 335 \\text{ remainder } 1. \\]\nThis means:\n\\[ 2011 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{6}. \\]\n\nThus,\n\\[ 6k + 2011 \\equiv 6k + 1 \\pmod{6}. \\]\n\nSince \\( 6k \\) is a multiple of 6, it does not affect the remainder when divided by 6. Therefore, the remainder when \\( 6k + 2011 \\) is divided by 6 is:\n\\[ 6k + 1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{6}. \\]\n\nSo, the remainder when \\( n + 2010 \\) is divided by 6 is \\(\\boxed{1}\\).",
